{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations","id":1554928,"title":"Newcomer needing informations","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-01T19:39:48-07:00","id":1554928,"title":"Newcomer needing informations","content":"I recently got my hands on 2nd editions of Werewolf the Forsaken and Vampire the Requiem, and maybe i missed it but they seem to lack some informations about explosives weapons (grenades, c4 or even car, truck and other source), and falling damage. Are those in another 2nd edition book?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-01T20:58:47-07:00","id":1554937,"title":"","content":"The layout decisions for 2e are such that a bunch of systems that are contained in the 1e nWoD corebook are not reproduced in the rules chapters of all 2e splat corebooks due to not being primary considerations in the design of those lines and/or space concerns leading to an alternative representation.<br /> <br /> 2e's mechanics for explosives and explosions are included in Hurt Locker (p134-137) and in Deviant: the Renegades (p204). The mechanics for falling damage can be found in the Chronicles of Darkness corebook (a.k.a the Revised Storytelling System Rulebook) (p97-98), Promethean 2e Core (p212-213), Changeling 2e Core (p190), Hunter 2e Core (p144), Geist 2e Core (p164), Mummy 2e Core (p189), Beast Core (p172), and Deviant Core.<br /> <br /> Basically almost every corebook for a gameline or new edition published after 2015 has the falling mechanics in it; Vampire, Werewolf, Mage, and Demon were all initially released while the IP was owned by CCP Games, who would not allow new editions to be made and thus were initially written as &quot;Chronicle books&quot; to be used with the 1e (new) World of Darkness corebook and the God-Machine Chronicle Rules Update to serve as a second edition in all but name, but the Chronicles of Darkness corebook wasn't compiled until after Vampire and Werewolf had updated Blood and Smoke: the Strix Chronicle and The Wolf Must Hunt: the Idigam Chronicle into Vampire: the Requiem Second Edition and Werewolf: the Forsaken Second Edition, respectively, by which point the layout decision had been made to include some iteration of the core mechanics as a dedicated chapter in each gameline's corebook.<br /> <br /> The specific format of that rules chapter has changed a couple times over the years, but the one in Deviant is both availing itself of the streamlined version that's been more or less used since Changeling 2e and incorporating the widest variety of additional systems for sources of harm and danger (such as drowning/suffocation, radiation, and acid, as well as separating Tilts beyond Poisoned and Sick into moderate and severe).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-02T07:03:27-08:00","id":1554957,"title":"","content":"So wich of those should i pick to get as much rules as possibles? Is there a book that compiled Falling damages, explosives damage environnemental hazards all at once?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-02T08:08:14-08:00","id":1554961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1554957#post1554957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So wich of those should i pick to get as much rules as possibles? Is there a book that compiled Falling damages, explosives damage environnemental hazards all at once?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Deviant: the Renegades corebook is the closest at the moment, Hurt Locker instead being built as a supplementary text \u2014 if you want rules on how to handle fighting while falling from extreme heights or how to use various non-combat Skills to do harm, Hurt Locker has you covered, but Deviant being a gameline corebook for a very diverse category of monster means it has necessarily prepared a lot of tools to that end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-02T09:50:34-08:00","id":1554971,"title":"","content":"Nice, thank a lot","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-09T14:57:34-08:00","id":1555288,"title":"","content":"I think you'll find those in the NWoD source-book, or the CoD, (2e).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-19T17:22:33-08:00","id":1555648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555288#post1555288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you'll find those in the NWoD source-book, or the CoD, (2e).</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll check those if i need more info, for now i try to get all the differences with masquerade. I was already a bit lost with morality with the three virtue in vtm but with Requiem there's none, only a number of dice you can roll depending on how low the sin of the pc is on the humanity.<br /> <br /> Honestly Humanity has always been a big interrogation for my party, it use some exemple of crime like stealing, killing or even selfish thoughts to tell if you must roll to lose humanity, but in the end it's a test to see if the character feel any guilt about this action, if yes he keep his dot if not he lose it. But morality can already vary from a culture or another and from a point in human history and our modern time, even one people let's say a trained soldier would not react the same when facing a threats as let's say a doctor.<br /> <br /> The whole point is not even the action itself but if the beast can access a bit more of freedom thanks to it, even VTM 20 tell that it seems hard to fall in the lower ranks of humanity so the st can simply tell that the character with 4 or lower act against his own will and roll conscience or spend willpower to restrain himself.<br /> <br /> I mean... why does THIS isn't the basic system? Telling player &quot;the bloodthirst is too strong, roll/spend to not drink all the blood of your prey&quot;, or &quot;Your ennemy is at your feet and killing him would be a waste of his power, roll/spend to not try to commit amaranth&quot; and so on, maybe even by giving negative condition to the player to tempt them to let the beast win this match to get rid of those conditions?<br /> <br /> Playing the fight against the inner beast more like an arm wrestling instead of a list of prohibitions that tends to make players paranoid on when will the st ask for a humanity check","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-19T17:59:55-08:00","id":1555650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555648#post1555648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'll check those if i need more info, for now i try to get all the differences with masquerade. I was already a bit lost with morality with the three virtue in vtm but with Requiem there's none, only a number of dice you can roll depending on how low the sin of the pc is on the humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>First of, while there are many similarities, overall I recommend to read this as a completely distinct game, because it is dissimilar enough that you may miss a lot of details if you don't read it as a new thing. Like how Requiem vampires has far better senses than VtM ones, with actual night vision and an astounding perception of blood as the main, but not sole, examples.<br /> <br /> CofD 2nd Edition does have a core mechanic for mortals that addresses how morality and experiences change. Integrity actually lets you define your own list of &quot;sins&quot;, here called Breaking Points. What keeps it balanced is that you actually get something out of rolling them, so how frequently you want to roll Integrity is actually an open question.<br /> <br /> Still, it is <i><b>extremely</b></i> important to notice the conceptual differences between VtM Humanity and VtR Humanity, and both and Integrity, which is also why they're now called Breaking Points. Integrity has more to do with resilience to stress and trauma, so to speak, than morality. It isn't about being good or bad, but about going through experiences that shake your sanity and/or emotional stability.<br /> <br /> Humanity in Requiem has to do with how much you remember how it is to be human and how to pretend to be one of them. Humanity Breaking Points may be crimes sometimes, but they're there less because they're bad things and more because they dampen your connection with humans, making you feel less a person and more a <i>thing of the night</i>. It doesn't take into account your actual feelings on this matter, either, truly monstrous Kindred may still want high Humanity because that makes easier for them to pretend and walk among the sheep, while a staunch defender of human rights may have low Humanity because that doesn't really means they feel any connection or similarity besides being sapient beings and they don't want to pretend to.<br /> <br /> That doesn't means VtR Humanity doesn't have some Gothic Morality undertones to it, but in general the idea stands, it isn't about being nice, but about having empathy with humans in the more technical definition of the word.<br /> <br /> All that also means that both Humanity and Integrity in CofD 2nd E have Breaking Points that are brought against you from the outside, not actions you take, and they're important to the game. A Mortal will frequently face Breaking Points through traumatic experiences with the supernatural regardless of what they chose to do about them. And Vampires suffer Breaking Points from things like reaching certain age thresholds, staying torpid for a long time or simply surviving humanly impossible injuries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-19T18:59:24-08:00","id":1555655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555648#post1555648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'll check those if i need more info, for now i try to get all the differences with masquerade. I was already a bit lost with morality with the three virtue in vtm but with Requiem there's none, only a number of dice you can roll depending on how low the sin of the pc is on the humanity.<br /> <br /> Honestly Humanity has always been a big interrogation for my party, it use some exemple of crime like stealing, killing or even selfish thoughts to tell if you must roll to lose humanity, but in the end it's a test to see if the character feel any guilt about this action, if yes he keep his dot if not he lose it. But morality can already vary from a culture or another and from a point in human history and our modern time, even one people let's say a trained soldier would not react the same when facing a threats as let's say a doctor.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Just think of morality is V:tM: Humanity; just you roll less dice and you don't have virtues anymore. And, in WoD/CoD, I think it's easier to lose humanity/morality, than in V:tM. <br /> <br /> And in other games, like D&amp;D, alignment, filled the role of humanity and dictated a character's moral-course. <br /> <br /> Another game, very similar to V:tM/WoD/CoD, is Cyberpunk: 2020; you have humanity, but, that's number that each new piece of cyberware you get, brings you closer to the beast; the beast in that game is being a mindless, Cyberpsycho. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-19T21:33:46-08:00","id":1555664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555650#post1555650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That doesn't means VtR Humanity doesn't have some Gothic Morality undertones to it, but in general the idea stands, it isn't about being nice, but about having empathy with humans in the more technical definition of the word.</div> </div> </div> </div>To draw a mechanical comparison to a later gameline, a vampire at Humanity 1 never rolls better than a chance die on any attempts to understand or relate to humans as anything other than food or threats, and this bears some similarities to the ways unbound geists in Geist: the Sin-Eater are constrained in exchange for their relative freedom of movement and deathly empowerment: the only Social rolls from and toward that aren't limited to a chance die are rolls to intimidate or frighten, and at the most tenuous connection to their Sin-Eater this is also the dicepool used to try and get it to not manifest in response to perceived or real threats and dangers.<br /> <br /> Compare also Mummy, whose Memory stat goes down when you act against the person you are now or deny the person you used to be and consequently bring yourself more in line with what the Judges you answer to want you to be, i.e. a brutal instrument of Bronze Age judgment and reappropriator of magical relics who moves only for the satisfaction of the avaricious urges of demonic gods of pain and misery.<br /> <br /> Or compare Demon, whose Cover stat wants you to be invested in <i>not</i> letting people connect true knowledge of your secret nature to the on-paper identity you occupy as a rogue archon on the run from the Demiurge that made you, or Changeling, whose Clarity stat contrariwise <i>really</i> wants you to not have to acknowledge the otherworldly life that turned you fae or be confronted with the sort of accidental gaslighting that happens when you insist to normal people that some stuff that doesn't even sound possible (and in many cases doesn't even sound like appealing fiction to the invested) happened to you.<br /> <br /> Three of these five gamelines had Rose Bailey prominently on their development team and I know she's spoken directly to at least two of them having the mechanics of their Integrity analogues tied up in the pressures of having to lie about what you are on a daily basis; perhaps relatedly, a trend that emerges as one reads through a lot of personal-scale text in Chronicles is that humanity is broadly aligned with the concept of denial or negation or falsehood and forces thereof, whether it's Heroes in Beast: the Primordial, the Spring Court in Changeling, the Abyssal shard in Mage, hunters in Hunter, or Requiem's deal with Humanity in general and the Strix's beef with it in particular.<br /> <br /> It's also notable that Humanity as an Integrity analog is a model mechanically designed to be lost down to a level the character can stabilize at, and that investment of effort (and Experiences) toward maintaining a higher level is something characters can choose to do or not bother doing. Some other monsters work similarly (notably including mages and hunters), while others start low and build higher as part of a progressing narrative (in Geist's case Synergy is actually a Supernatural Potency stat with loss conditions, whereas in Promethean and Mummy Pilgrimage and Memory the character is pursuing understanding of humanity and themselves, respectively) and still others have less entropic models like two-way scales (e.g. Harmony from Werewolf or Satiety from Beast) or damage tracks (Clarity from Changeling or Stability from Deviant: the Renegades).<br /> <br /> Requiem loosely attaches becoming a worse person to becoming <i>worse at being &quot;a person,&quot;</i> but it also provides the loss of your original human context as another route to that slow slide down the ladder, whether it's the eventual death of everyone who knew you or the encountering a culture that didn't exist when you were alive or just the simple cognitive dissonance of being confronted with the fact that you're dead even though you're walking around (a similar realization also being one of the few things that can cause ghosts to lose Integrity after their death, as well). The moral metaphysics of the Chronicles of Darkness are such that the fabric of a character's identity as a participant in human society and the world tends to react poorly to contradictions of that identity, Walt Whitman quotes about containing multitudes be damned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-19T23:20:34-08:00","id":1555673,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555655#post1555655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another game, very similar to V:tM/WoD/CoD, is Cyberpunk: 2020; you have humanity, but, that's number that each new piece of cyberware you get, brings you closer to the beast; the beast in that game is being a mindless, Cyberpsycho.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't really compare Cyberpsychosis that much to VtM Humanity. I mean, there's something to it, but also a lot of other themes.<br /> <br /> The actual source of those systems, both from WoD, CofD, Cyberpunk 20XX and other RPGs, is Chaosium's <i>Call of Cthulhu</i> Insanity System. It is arguably among the most copied and influential pieces of rule in RPG history.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-20T06:32:35-08:00","id":1555682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555650#post1555650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Humanity in Requiem has to do with how much you remember how it is to be human and how to pretend to be one of them. Humanity Breaking Points may be crimes sometimes, but they're there less because they're bad things and more because they dampen your connection with humans, making you feel less a person and more a <i>thing of the night</i>. It doesn't take into account your actual feelings on this matter, either, truly monstrous Kindred may still want high Humanity because that makes easier for them to pretend and walk among the sheep, while a staunch defender of human rights may have low Humanity because that doesn't really means they feel any connection or similarity besides being sapient beings and they don't want to pretend to.<br /> <br /> That doesn't means VtR Humanity doesn't have some Gothic Morality undertones to it, but in general the idea stands, it isn't about being nice, but about having empathy with humans in the more technical definition of the word.<br /> <br /> All that also means that both Humanity and Integrity in CofD 2nd E have Breaking Points that are brought against you from the outside, not actions you take, and they're important to the game. A Mortal will frequently face Breaking Points through traumatic experiences with the supernatural regardless of what they chose to do about them. And Vampires suffer Breaking Points from things like reaching certain age thresholds, staying torpid for a long time or simply surviving humanly impossible injuries.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually i understood that it depend on how the character may react to loosing sight of his human self depending on trauma, i just say that not everyone may have a breaking point for the same thing, that means that a pc can have a breaking point by let's say killing another vampire to protect himself or something/someone (a doctor or a pacifist...) while another pc would not (a former human trained soldier)<br /> This mean that the st should take in account each pc persona individually instead of using the general hierarchy of sin in the book.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555650#post1555650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Integrity actually lets you define your own list of &quot;sins&quot;, here called Breaking Points</div> </div> </div> </div> So either the pc hand their breaking points to the st or declare them when the scene and their action depend on them, or either the st ask for humanity check only when there's really something difficult happening to the pc (loosing something important, a person they love, a territory, being hunted by other vampire for a crime they did or didn't commit, seeing their human friends aging while staying alive...) wich would be story guided instead of tracking each decision of the pc<br /> <br /> That would make humanity work a bit like rage for werewolf, when they live traumatic experience they may buy a rage dot, while vampire when living traumatic experience may lose a dot (now that i think about it werewolves cannot solve their trauma to reduce their rage in raw, however it make their life harder with human interaction and more vulnerable to frenzy but it's not the subject here)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-20T08:52:11-08:00","id":1555686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555682#post1555682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This mean that the st should take in account each pc persona individually instead of using the general hierarchy of sin in the book.</div> </div> </div> </div>So the thing is, Chronicles of Darkness does not generally support wholly removing incentives against killing from its Integrity systems for characters who aren't extremely spiritually divergent from normal humanity (and even then). Personally-defined breaking points are generally in addition to the ones printed in the book and the overwhelming majority of human cultures socialize their members to attach a negative connotation to the general act of killing people and build on exceptions later that, mechanically, may make the roll more likely to have a positive result. Gaining new banes simply doesn't practically work otherwise.<br /> <br /> This isn't like Morality, where people could make arguments about the severity of different degrees of assault and theft \u2014 a vampire who has deadened themselves to the impact of killing someone in a fit of passion no longer takes the Bestial, Competitive, or Wanton Condition when they do so, but deliberately killing someone is still bad for the Humanity of most vampires who have the average Humanity rating for their age unless they've warped their souls and taken on weird folkloric weaknesses that nevertheless get less severe the better they are at pretending they're human. (And that specific division is likewise important for mages, who reverse the order of planned and impassioned murder on their own ladder because Wisdom is concerned with self-control over a process that can operate subconsciously.)<br /> <br /> I get that player psychology tends to be risk-averse and loss-averse and Humanity costing Experiences to raise makes it feel bad to roll for Detachment if you aren't gleefully along for the ride as the character degenerates, but unless you are killing people all the time this is a problem that literally solves itself once you've managed to restabilize your night-to-night unliving situation and that's part of the reason vampires build sociopolitical machines and make ghouls and thralls and carefully cultivate the spaces they move through and rest in.<br /> <br /> Being forced to do illegal, immoral, and/or unethical stuff is the domain of the desperate, while going out of your way to do it is the stuff of the terminally overprivileged. Neither is good for maintaining relatability to the average living member of your society.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-20T20:20:42-08:00","id":1555700,"title":"","content":"First of all, in addition to what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Satchel</a> said, and considering that narrative and thematic relevance take precedence over everything else, I want to add something as a Psychologist:<br /> <br /> Killing isn't a matter of morality.<br /> <br /> Most societies are against killing outside very specific situations, either as law or just a moral standing, but beyond that there's a mental block against killing we're simply wired with as humans.<br /> <br /> Of course this doesn't means we can't kill. We very clearly can. It also don't mean killing is always immoral, it very clearly isn't. But it does mean that for a non-disabled person in that area (like <i>some</i> sociopaths and psychopaths), killing is <i><b>ALWAYS</b></i> traumatic, no exceptions.<br /> <br /> This has even been one of the biggest conundrums of military training through human history. Most fatalities in battles don't actually occur from directly fatal injuries, because very few soldiers can actually force themselves to cause such injuries. The flinch, they pull back, they cause non-fatal injuries if they can. It's all nice and dandy at training and talking, but to actually do it is a lot harder. Most soldiers actually survive loosing battles, and the deaths that do occur are usually due to infected injuries. More soldiers fall to other causes rather than enemy steel.<br /> <br /> And one of the reasons experienced soldiers are valuable is because they're better at getting to do the deed. That's also a big source of trauma for soldiers, and one of the reasons the US military has one of the worse outcomes for veteran mental health is because it cracked the secret to significantly increase disposition to kill. This works, but takes a severe toll on the soldiers (which are then released in society mentally broken, as many veterans know).<br /> <br /> So at the same time that it is an important narrative element the idea that killing is never without trauma, or ok when you're at a very bad place already, it's also actually very real that killing is always traumatic regardless of moral standing or previous deeds. And remember, the main point here is trauma, not morality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555682#post1555682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually i understood that it depend on how the character may react to loosing sight of his human self depending on trauma, i just say that not everyone may have a breaking point for the same thing, that means that a pc can have a breaking point by let's say killing another vampire to protect himself or something/someone (a doctor or a pacifist...) while another pc would not (a former human trained soldier)<br /> This mean that the st should take in account each pc persona individually instead of using the general hierarchy of sin in the book.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem here is two-fold.<br /> <br /> First, the overall idea is to have a distinct Integrity-like system that works with the themes of the splat, so avoiding to do the same mortals do is part of the point. The Hierarchy of Sins in Vampire is meant to work within the narrative themes of the game and reinforce just how much a Vampire both is a former human, but is also drifting from it, becoming something else.<br /> <br /> Second, the very fact that it exists to deal with a non-human psyche and a specific sort of consequence. This is not about trauma in a general sense, this is specifically about the kind of shock that may deaden the connection of the character to a human condition they don't really have anymore.<br /> <br /> How a Vampire reacts to emotional trauma in general is given by their behavior, but the very fact they don't have an Integrity anymore and won't suffer the Conditions a mortal suffers for those things is telling. Humanity covers things the character may not really give a damn about, they're not trauma in the sense of being emotionally shocking, but in that they affect their memory of what it's like to be physiologically alive and eat food, instead of being a predator that sucks blood from the living.<br /> <br /> In a sense, it's the very fact that the character doesn't suffer a <i>regular</i> Breaking Point from murder anymore that makes them susceptible to a Humanity Breaking Point when doing it. It's not their emotional, moral or rational stance on the act of killing, but the fact that it <u><i>feels</i></u> like killing a vermin, now, and different of a human no amount of thinking this is bad will make it <b>feel</b> bad.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555682#post1555682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So either the pc hand their breaking points to the st or declare them when the scene and their action depend on them, or either the st ask for humanity check only when there's really something difficult happening to the pc (loosing something important, a person they love, a territory, being hunted by other vampire for a crime they did or didn't commit, seeing their human friends aging while staying alive...) wich would be story guided instead of tracking each decision of the pc</div> </div> </div> </div>On top of the above considerations, there's the point that asking this for a non-human psyche is far harder on both players and the ST than regular Integrity. You need a more solid framework to deal with the fact that this <b>isn't Integrity</b> and isn't about how humans work.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555686#post1555686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being forced to do illegal, immoral, and/or unethical stuff is the domain of the desperate, while going out of your way to do it is the stuff of the terminally overprivileged. Neither is good for maintaining relatability to the average living member of your society.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here's another very particular diference between VtR and VtM.<br /> <br /> While the system itself has more ways to cause &quot;sins&quot;, the setting has actually less pressure on the characters for doing so against their will.<br /> <br /> In most chronicles keeping Humanity at whatever level you want is pretty doable. You'll have the eventual shake up, but actually loosing it is rare enough that buying back isn't as big a deal as it seems.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T17:21:55-08:00","id":1555749,"title":"","content":"That's very instructive, maybe too much because all that has to be understood and converted in rules you can deal with during the game, in that sense taking a practicle exemple may be of some use<br /> <br /> Let's say you take a vampire, we'll call him Tom, that is a pc. Since the whole point of keeping his humanity is to remember what's being human mean, to preserve that feeling to keep the beast at bay, there's a lot of thing that a vampire need that can make him forget; Power from disciplines and aging, seeing humans aging and dying while staying in the same, satisfying the bloodthirst, observing the world change and become something you don't recognise anymore, being unable to have some intimate relationship with the living since the body is dead unless by imitating life by spending blood (blush of life), facing supernatural menace that any regular mortal would only see in their nightmares, killing or harming another to survive and so on<br /> <br /> When does a humanity check is needed in this existence? Some reason could be some scenario plot like seeing time go by and loosing humans contact due to old age, but others like facing supernatural menace, drinking blood, fighting to death or suffering wound that would killl mortals are more immediat events.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T17:36:48-08:00","id":1555775,"title":"","content":"Both in CP:2020, and in V:tM, you can lose yourself to a beast.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T19:36:52-08:00","id":1555781,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1554928-newcomer-needing-informations?p=1555749#post1555749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When does a humanity check is needed in this existence? Some reason could be some scenario plot like seeing time go by and loosing humans contact due to old age, but others like facing supernatural menace, drinking blood, fighting to death or suffering wound that would killl mortals are more immediat events.</div> </div> </div> </div>Neonates and other higher-Humanity vampires check for Detachment fairly often and with decent odds of success (commonplace breaking points at that level roll three or four dice, and you'll likely have a Touchstone or two attached as well to add two or three more to the roll, plus the chance to play into your Dirge for another die).<br /> <br /> Detachment checks for surviving very long spans of time or losing a lot of time to torpor are functionally ways of enforcing the natural decline in Humanity with age and disincentivizing Kindred from putting themselves in harm's way, but unless you're anticipating more breaking points at your current level or a need for more Touchstones (whether as an extra source of Willpower or as more ways to manage the risks of frenzy-related Conditions), you can just settle at your new level \u2014 replacing Touchstones requires buying dots of Humanity, but the reverse is not the case.<br /> <br /> Remember that there's an entire diceless feeding system that boils down to &quot;take turns describing events (dictating other characters' actions is allowed) and the spotlight player can take full narrative control by invoking their Anchors, but if they do so more than [6-current Humanity] times in the scene they check for Detachment at the end,&quot; which ludonarratively translates to &quot;characters with lower Humanity scores can assert themselves more in the diceless feeding system without needing to risk losing their remaining Humanity&quot; and &quot;the diceless feeding system gives characters with higher Humanity lower-risk Beats for going with the flow.&quot;<br /> <br /> You could, if you were so inclined, run a character's passing their hundred-year mark as a vignette, but for practical purposes most of the Detachment checks a character will need to worry about are those tied to their immediate actions, major decisions, or abrupt mishaps.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Fixed a typo. 11-23-2025, 11:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}